WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.115
I'm David Haussler, scientific director

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:18.651
of the UC Santa Cruz Genomics Institute,

00:00:18.652 --> 00:00:21.454
and Howard Hughes Medical Institute investigator.

00:00:21.455 --> 00:00:24.891
It's my pleasure to be able to speak to you

00:00:24.892 --> 00:00:27.460
on the great questions in biology.

00:00:27.461 --> 00:00:32.598
I want to have you imagine

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:35.234
that you just got your genome sequenced.

00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:37.737
Now, when we sequenced the first genome

00:00:37.738 --> 00:00:41.941
in the year 2000, it cost about $300 million,

00:00:41.942 --> 00:00:44.877
just for the sequencing reagents and activities.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:47.513
But nowadays, it's a couple thousand bucks.

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:52.652
So let's assume, you went out and got your sequence done.

00:00:52.653 --> 00:00:58.824
Now looking at that, it may be hard to interpret

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:03.162
but it's got to be a moving experience,

00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:06.365
to look at that DNA sequence and think about

00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:08.968
how it got there.

00:01:08.969 --> 00:01:13.072
In particular the DNA in your genome

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:16.442
was passed on from generation to generation

00:01:16.443 --> 00:01:18.211
for eons.

00:01:18.212 --> 00:01:21.881
We all come out of the primordial ooze somewhere

00:01:21.882 --> 00:01:24.050
billions of years ago

00:01:24.051 --> 00:01:27.453
And it's stunning to think that a record

00:01:27.454 --> 00:01:29.889
of many of those evolutionary events

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:33.159
is still present in the genomes today.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:38.664
So what would you do? What would you look a

00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:41.734
to start to understand where you came from?

00:01:41.735 --> 00:01:46.572
And what is specifically interesting about your genome

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:49.408
versus all the other genomes on the planet?

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:54.647
Probably the first thing you would think about

00:01:54.648 --> 00:01:57.884
in terms of the way that DNA is inherited

00:01:57.885 --> 00:02:00.586
from parent to offspring is:

00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:04.223
are there any new elements of your genome

00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:05.825
that weren't even in your parents?

00:02:05.826 --> 00:02:09.128
And statistics say there will be a few

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:12.665
So there will be a few changes in the way the DNA

00:02:12.666 --> 00:02:16.169
was copied. You could think of them as errors,

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:19.605
but you could also think of them as fortuitous events

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:23.176
that caused something different in your genome

00:02:23.177 --> 00:02:25.044
that wasn't in either of your parent's genome.

00:02:25.045 --> 00:02:27.747
Those would be interesting, certainly.

00:02:27.748 --> 00:02:32.051
And there are probably only a very small handful of those.

00:02:32.052 --> 00:02:34.854
The next thing you would think about is

00:02:34.855 --> 00:02:40.326
is there something I inherited from my parents

00:02:40.327 --> 00:02:43.863
that's just specific to my family in some sense?

00:02:43.864 --> 00:02:47.567
Maybe it's something special that happened in

00:02:47.568 --> 00:02:51.537
a great grandparent and has been passed down

00:02:51.538 --> 00:02:53.840
to me through all of these generations.

00:02:53.841 --> 00:02:57.844
Now this is the very stuff of genetics.

00:02:57.845 --> 00:03:01.013
To think about this, in particular, medical genetics

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:04.650
you would be very concerned if this actually

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:05.785
made you prone to a disease

00:03:05.786 --> 00:03:10.489
You might also be protected from a disease

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:13.626
by a special version of a gene that's in your genome

00:03:13.627 --> 00:03:17.897
that's specific, or private, to your specific family.

00:03:17.898 --> 00:03:21.133
That would be exciting.

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:26.105
And as we start to get the era from just one genome

00:03:26.106 --> 00:03:30.042
in the year 2000, to the coming era of millions of genomes,

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:33.679
we will be able to, by comparing genomes,

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.850
understand what's specific to certain families,

00:03:37.851 --> 00:03:40.019
what's specific to certain ethnic groups,

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:46.025
what's specific to humans in general, but not

00:03:46.026 --> 00:03:47.527
with other species.

00:03:47.528 --> 00:03:50.830
It's an enormous computational problem

00:03:50.831 --> 00:03:53.933
to compare all of these genomes, and this is probably

00:03:53.934 --> 00:03:59.071
the most significant challenge facing the computational part

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:01.908
of genetics and genomics today.

00:04:01.909 --> 00:04:05.945
If you understood what was specific to humans

00:04:05.946 --> 00:04:08.915
that would be fascinating, you could start to think about

00:04:08.916 --> 00:04:12.718
what happened since we diverged from our common ancestor

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:16.989
with our closest species, the neanderthal.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:19.825
While the neanderthals are extinct,

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:23.863
we were able to sequence DNA from their bones

00:04:23.864 --> 00:04:27.466
and hence, get an idea of their genomes looked like.

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:32.171
And we find that there are more than a million changes

00:04:32.172 --> 00:04:35.141
that occurred in the human lineage

00:04:35.142 --> 00:04:37.877
since we diverged from our common ancestor

00:04:37.878 --> 00:04:39.212
to neanderthal.

00:04:39.213 --> 00:04:42.448
What an exciting project at this point, to try to understand

00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:48.087
those changes that almost everybody

00:04:48.088 --> 00:04:50.022
almost all of humankind share,

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:55.061
as opposed to and distinguish them from the neanderthal.

00:04:55.062 --> 00:05:01.467
Going back further, about 5-6 million years ago,

00:05:01.468 --> 00:05:04.136
we shared a common ancestor with the chimpanzee

00:05:04.137 --> 00:05:08.074
Since that time, there have been roughly 15 million changes

00:05:08.075 --> 00:05:09.809
in our genome.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:13.045
Which ones actually account for the difference

00:05:13.046 --> 00:05:14.647
between a human and a chimp?

00:05:14.648 --> 00:05:17.049
This is a substantial difference

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:20.286
and remarkably, we still know very little

00:05:20.287 --> 00:05:25.458
about which of those changes actually make that huge difference

00:05:25.459 --> 00:05:27.460
between a human and a chimp.

00:05:27.461 --> 00:05:31.531
One thing that you'll run into, in this quest,

00:05:31.532 --> 00:05:35.868
is the fact that most of these changes

00:05:35.869 --> 00:05:39.438
are probably not important

00:05:39.439 --> 00:05:40.940
in some sense.

00:05:40.941 --> 00:05:45.611
If you look at the structure of your DNA

00:05:45.612 --> 00:05:48.814
and in particular, if you look at it from this perspective

00:05:48.815 --> 00:05:52.885
of going back and looking at where the DNA came from

00:05:52.886 --> 00:05:54.620
what it's history is,

00:05:54.621 --> 00:05:57.290
you see that there are some parts of your genome

00:05:57.291 --> 00:06:00.726
that are shared with virtually all other life

00:06:00.727 --> 00:06:02.061
on the planet.

00:06:02.062 --> 00:06:04.163
The genes and the DNA in the genes

00:06:04.164 --> 00:06:06.032
and the ribosome sequences,

00:06:06.033 --> 00:06:11.604
DNA polymerase. Other fundamental molecules

00:06:11.605 --> 00:06:13.439
that make life itself possible

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.608
are remarkably little changed

00:06:15.609 --> 00:06:18.244
over the eons of evolutionary time.

00:06:18.245 --> 00:06:22.114
And so when you look at one of those bases in your genome

00:06:22.115 --> 00:06:27.019
you can sit back and say, "wow, that's a really ancient base!"

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:31.090
"That base of DNA was passed on to me

00:06:31.091 --> 00:06:34.227
all the way back from the beginning

00:06:34.228 --> 00:06:37.363
billions of years, copied faithfully

00:06:37.364 --> 00:06:38.898
again and again and again.

00:06:38.899 --> 00:06:42.001
And now it is a gift to me

00:06:42.002 --> 00:06:45.438
so that my cells work."

00:06:45.439 --> 00:06:51.410
In between that are all stages of evolutionary innovation.

00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:53.446
So when you look at your genome

00:06:53.447 --> 00:06:58.818
you'll find genes that were created essentially

00:06:58.819 --> 00:07:03.222
as an evolutionary process in the bilaterian animals,

00:07:03.223 --> 00:07:04.657
for example.

00:07:04.658 --> 00:07:08.194
The set of animals that have bilateral symmetry

00:07:08.195 --> 00:07:11.330
is a huge collection of animals on the planet

00:07:11.331 --> 00:07:14.000
that they didn't exist 2 billion years ago.

00:07:14.001 --> 00:07:17.803
So all of their genetic innovations happened

00:07:17.804 --> 00:07:19.639
after that period.

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:21.240
If you look at vertebrates,

00:07:21.241 --> 00:07:22.842
animals with a backbone,

00:07:22.843 --> 00:07:28.247
they didn't exist 800 million years ago, but now

00:07:28.248 --> 00:07:31.551
we can find all of the different innovations

00:07:31.552 --> 00:07:33.653
that are specific to the vertebrates,

00:07:33.654 --> 00:07:36.522
the backboned animals, that don't exist in other animals.

00:07:36.523 --> 00:07:40.126
And each one of these beautiful genetic variations

00:07:40.127 --> 00:07:42.628
happened at a particular time

00:07:42.629 --> 00:07:44.564
in the marvelous history of life.

00:07:44.565 --> 00:07:47.600
So when you're looking at every gene in your genome,

00:07:47.601 --> 00:07:51.537
you can say "Aha! That's a bilaterian innovation."

00:07:51.538 --> 00:07:55.041
"Oh, and this one was invented by vertebrates."

00:07:55.042 --> 00:07:58.377
"And maybe this one was invented by primates."

00:07:58.378 --> 00:08:01.214
"And maybe this one is specific to apes."

00:08:01.215 --> 00:08:08.020
Understanding this, is probably the greatest challenge

00:08:08.021 --> 00:08:12.124
to genomics, going forward at this point.

00:08:12.125 --> 00:08:14.427
And we have an extraordinary opportunity

00:08:14.428 --> 00:08:17.663
to look at every base in the genome

00:08:17.664 --> 00:08:19.432
for the first time.

00:08:19.433 --> 00:08:23.269
And more importantly, to compare it

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:25.705
to the bases in other genomes.

00:08:25.706 --> 00:08:30.643
The lesson we've learned from first sequencing the human genome

00:08:30.644 --> 00:08:34.080
in the year 2000, and subsequently looking

00:08:34.081 --> 00:08:35.815
at the first chimpanzee genome,

00:08:35.816 --> 00:08:37.350
the first mouse genome,

00:08:37.351 --> 00:08:39.085
the first rat genome,

00:08:39.086 --> 00:08:40.753
the first dog genome,

00:08:40.754 --> 00:08:44.790
is that no genome is ever understandable

00:08:44.791 --> 00:08:45.925
in isolation.

00:08:45.926 --> 00:08:49.362
Every time we sequence the genome of a new species

00:08:49.363 --> 00:08:53.466
we learn more about the genomes that we had previously

00:08:53.467 --> 00:08:55.801
sequenced from other species.

00:08:55.802 --> 00:08:58.204
And that is precisely because

00:08:58.205 --> 00:09:00.406
we share a common heritage

00:09:00.407 --> 00:09:03.776
and because we are sculpted by evolution.

00:09:03.777 --> 00:09:07.446
By looking at these patterns of conservation,

00:09:07.447 --> 00:09:09.148
and change within our genome,

00:09:09.149 --> 00:09:13.786
we can often decode something about the function

00:09:13.787 --> 00:09:15.555
of a region of DNA.

00:09:15.556 --> 00:09:19.659
For example, if it codes for protein sequence,

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:23.396
then it has to have this triplet pattern of codons

00:09:23.397 --> 00:09:27.400
and you'll find that while there are changes in the region

00:09:27.401 --> 00:09:31.571
they preserve this fundamental property of being able to code

00:09:31.572 --> 00:09:34.407
for a protein, and we can see that clearly

00:09:34.408 --> 00:09:36.475
in the pattern of changes which are allowed

00:09:36.476 --> 00:09:38.578
or not allowed.

00:09:38.579 --> 00:09:41.214
So that gives us a window,

00:09:41.215 --> 00:09:45.218
just by studying comparisons between many pieces of DNA,

00:09:45.219 --> 00:09:48.154
into the function of those pieces of DNA.

00:09:48.155 --> 00:09:52.024
But we're only seeing the tip of the iceberg here.

00:09:52.025 --> 00:09:53.993
We're very much at the beginning

00:09:53.994 --> 00:09:58.030
of a long journey, now that we're in the genomics era

00:09:58.031 --> 00:10:00.900
and being able to look at all of these genomes

00:10:00.901 --> 00:10:03.970
together, of decoding them through

00:10:03.971 --> 00:10:06.572
their history, through comparison.

00:10:06.573 --> 00:10:09.709
I hope you will consider taking this journey

00:10:09.710 --> 00:10:15.147
with us. It's a journey that needs not only biologists,

00:10:15.148 --> 00:10:16.415
but computer scientists.

00:10:16.416 --> 00:10:19.151
Right now the world is struggling

00:10:19.152 --> 00:10:21.654
to be able to write the software code

00:10:21.655 --> 00:10:23.589
to create the computer architecture

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:26.659
to be able to compare the full genomes

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:30.963
from hundreds, or thousands, of different species

00:10:30.964 --> 00:10:36.068
This is the very edge of our capabilities at this point.

00:10:36.069 --> 00:10:39.438
And we can look forward to great innovations,

00:10:39.439 --> 00:10:44.143
both in terms of computers and algorithms,

00:10:44.144 --> 00:10:47.246
big data, cloud computing, all of these

00:10:47.247 --> 00:10:51.217
things will have a say, along with traditional

00:10:51.218 --> 00:10:55.054
fields like biology, molecular biology, biochemistry,

00:10:55.055 --> 00:10:58.524
and evolution population genetics.

00:10:58.525 --> 00:11:01.961
So it's a great time to be involved in this,

00:11:01.962 --> 00:11:04.531
and I invite you to this wonderful adventure.

